USER GUIDE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ABOUT DINOLINO:

DinoLino is a Windows program for 2D geometric morphometrics. It measures shape from photographs of specimens: curvature, angles, distances, and outlines.

Each image opened is treated as a specimen that keeps its own scale, alignment, picture corrections, and measurements. Operations are drawn in Curvature Mode, Angle Mode, Draw Mode, or Outline Mode, and the operation history can be exported as an .xlsx workbook for statistical analysis.

The version number and list of contributors are in the About window of the Help menu.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROJECTS:

The five project commands are at the top of the File menu: New Project, Open Project, Save Project and Save Project As. A project is saved as a .dlino file.

A project is a whole session in one file: every specimen with its picture, scale, alignment and picture corrections, every measurement together with the geometry behind it, the outlines kept as silhouettes, and the table state that goes with them. That last part covers the group columns, the formula columns, the variables chosen for the Custom table, the columns hidden from a table, and the tables staged for the workbook.

Save Project:
Writes the session to a project file. The first save asks where to put it, and later saves go back to the same file. Save Project As writes the session to another file and carries on in that one.

Open Project:
Replaces the session with the one in the file. Anything the current session holds that has not been saved is offered a save first.

New Project:
Empties the session and starts again, offering the same save first.

Autosave:
While a session holds anything a project file does not, DinoLino keeps a copy of it every few minutes, and again when the program closes. The copy is kept with the program's settings rather than beside the project, so a session that has never been saved is kept as well.

Closing the program asks nothing. If there was work outstanding, the next start offers it back, naming the project it belonged to and when it was kept. Saving the project, starting a new one and opening another all remove the kept copy, and so does turning the offer down.

Pictures:
A photograph is referenced by where it sits on disk rather than copied into the project, which keeps the project file small. The path is read relative to the project first, so a project and its photographs can be moved together as long as they keep the same arrangement. A picture that no file on disk reproduces is stored inside the project instead: a view captured from a 3D model, or an image rotated or flipped since it was opened.

A photograph that cannot be found when a project opens is named, and its specimen comes back with its measurements but no picture. Opening that image again brings the picture back.

The title bar shows which project the session belongs to, with a star beside the name while the session holds something the project file does not.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GLOBAL METHODS:

Undo:	
Undoes the last action performed by the user. This returns the metadata of the previous calculation and removes the last drawn operation.

Redo:	
Redoes the last action that was undone by the user. This returns the metadata of the next calculation and redraws the operation.

Clear Specimen Measurements:
Takes every measurement and every label off the specimen on screen and blanks every mode's results, not only those of the mode you are in. Anything you have undone but not yet redone goes with them. The specimen itself, its image and its scale are kept, and other specimens are untouched. This cannot be undone.
The same command is on the Clear Specimen Measurements button in the sidebar, on Ctrl + Shift + C, and on the Delete specimen button beside the loaded specimen in a Data Workshop edit window. All four ask the same question, and it names the specimen and how many recorded operations are about to go.
The question carries a "Do not show this message again" box. Ticking it and clearing stops the question being asked for the rest of the session, and for good if File, Save Settings is on; ticking it and cancelling changes nothing. File, Restore Default Settings brings the question back.
To clear every specimen at once, use File, New Project, which offers to save first.

Zoom:
Use the scroll wheel to zoom in and out of the image at the location of the cursor.

Drag image:
Hold Ctrl and left click to drag and move the viewable window while zoomed in.

Move cursor:
Move the mouse, or hold Ctrl and press arrow keys to nudge the cursor one pixel at a time. Hold Shift and press arrow keys to move in larger steps.

Press the spacebar to left click at the cursor's position.

Averages:
The Avg column beside each output is the mean of that measurement across all operations of the same kind for the current specimen.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS: 

Undo: 					Ctrl + Z
Redo: 					Ctrl + Y
Clear Specimen Measurements: 		Ctrl + Shift + C
Copy selected text: 		Ctrl + C
Open image: 			Ctrl + F
Open 3D model:			Ctrl + 3
New project:			Ctrl + N
Open project:			Ctrl + O
Save project:			Ctrl + Shift + S
Edit history:			Ctrl + H
Export history: 		Ctrl + E
Take screenshot: 		Ctrl + S
Finish spline:			Enter
Cancel:					Esc
View next specimen:		Up arrow key
View previous specimen: Down arrow key
Zoom:					Scroll wheel (mouse) or pinch (trackpad)
Scroll sideways:		Shift + scroll wheel, or a two-finger sideways swipe
Drag image:				Ctrl + left click and drag
Move cursor slowly:		Ctrl + arrow keys
Move cursor quickly:	Shift + arrow keys
Left click:				Spacebar (hold to drag)
Double click:			Spacebar (twice rapidly)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CURVATURE MODE: 

Geometric calculations to calculate curvature 

----- Circular Arc -----

Approximate a curve as the arc of a circle using three points. 
Click twice to draw a line from each end of the curve (your 'chord'), then click a third time in the midpoint of the curve to draw the chord bisector (your 'sagitta'). 
Click again after arc completion to begin another operation.

Central Angle: A measure of curvature. The angle, in degrees, subtended at the center of the circle passing through all three points.
Chord-Arc Ratio: A measure of curvature. The length of the first line drawn (chord) divided by arc length. Arc length = radius * central angle (in radians).
Rise-Span ratio: Length of the chord bisector (sagitta) divided by length of the first line drawn (chord).
Radius: Radius of the circle passing through all three points.

----- Parabolic Arc -----

Approximate a curve as a parabola using three points.
Click twice to draw a line from each end of the curve (your 'chord'), then click a third time in the midpoint of the curve to draw the chord bisector (your 'sagitta'). 
Click again after arc completion to begin another operation.

The parabola y = ax^2 + bx + c is fitted through the three points with the chord scaled to unit length. Arc length is measured along 64 points sampled on the fitted parabola.

Chord-Arc Ratio: A measure of curvature. The length of the first line drawn (chord) divided by arc length.
Rise-Span Ratio: Length of the chord bisector (sagitta) divided by the length of the chord.
Vertex Curvature: Curvature at the vertex of the fitted parabola, equal to 2*a, taken as an absolute value. Dimensionless, because the chord is scaled to unit length.
Vertex Radius: Radius of curvature at the vertex, equal to chord length divided by vertex curvature.

----- n-Point Spline -----

Choose Catmull-Rom, Bezier, or Freehand, then draw along the curve. Beginning a new curve after one is finished starts another operation.

Catmull-Rom splines: The spline must pass through every point. This operation uses centripetal Catmull-Rom splines, which are well-suited for non-uniform biological shapes. Click any number of times along the curve, then press 'Enter' to finish.
Bezier splines: The spline is pulled towards the clicked points, but does not necessarily pass through them. This operation uses Schneider's Bezier fitting, with a tolerance of 2 pixels, to convert n points into one or more smooth cubic Bezier segments. Click any number of times along the curve, then press 'Enter' to finish.
Freehand: Click, hold the left mouse button, and drag along the curve. The spline is finished when the mouse button is released.

Splines are measured along 50 sampled points per segment.

Turn.Angles/Length: A measure of curvature. The sum of the turning angles (absolute change in angle of the tangent lines along the spline, in degrees) divided by the length of the spline. Approximates the average change in angle of the tangent lines along the spline.
Tortuosity: The length of the spline divided by the length of the straight line (chord) connecting its start and end points. Equal to 1 for a straight line.
Length: Length of the spline.

Freehand Smoothing:
Slider from 0 to 10, default 2. Filters hand tremor from the drawn stroke. Higher values give a smoother curve but pull it away from fine detail.
The stroke is resampled to points spaced 4 pixels apart, smoothed with a Gaussian filter of width 0.5 * Smoothing (in samples), and simplified with the Ramer-Douglas-Peucker algorithm at a tolerance of 2 + Smoothing pixels. A centripetal Catmull-Rom spline is fitted through the remaining points. At 0, no smoothing is applied.

----- Find Turning Angle / Length -----

Measures Turn.Angles/Length over a section of the most recently drawn spline instead of the whole curve. Move the cursor along the spline to choose the section. Section width sets the number of samples on each side of the cursor. Press Esc to exit.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ANGLE MODE:

Measures angles, in either of two ways. Choose one with the radio buttons at the top of the panel.

----- Triangle -----
Draws a triangle on the image and returns its three interior angles and proportions. 

Click three times anywhere on the image to create a triangle. Click again to begin drawing another triangle.

Angle A:		The angle at vertex A.
Angle B:		The angle at vertex B.
Angle C:		The angle at vertex C.
Aspect Ratio:	Length of the longest side of the triangle divided by its height.
Area:			Area of the triangle.
Relative Size:	Area of the current triangle divided by the area of the previous triangle.

Note: Triangle height is the perpendicular distance from its longest side to the opposite vertex.

----- Axis Angle -----
Measures which way a feature points, against the specimen's own axis rather than against another line.

Click two points along the feature. The angle is measured clockwise from the specimen's X axis to the line drawn, over a full turn, so a feature pointing one way and the same feature pointing back read as different angles. A second click too close to the first is ignored rather than measured.

Set the axis first with Tools, Align, Align Specimen. Without one the angle is measured from the image's own axis instead, which depends on how the photograph happened to be rotated. The line beneath the reading says which of the two is in use, and the table records which one each angle was taken against.

Angle:			Clockwise angle from the specimen's axis to the line drawn.

The average is the mean direction, taken round the circle rather than as a plain average, so angles either side of the axis average to the direction between them.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRAW MODE:

Draw basic shapes and lines on the image and return their relative proportions.

----- Draw Shapes -----
Choose a rectangle, square, ellipse, or circle. Click once to begin drawing a geometric shape, drag to determine size and shape, and click a second time to finish.

Area: Area of the drawn shape.
Aspect Ratio: Width of the drawn shape divided by its height. Rectangles and ellipses only.
Relative Size: Area of the drawn shape divided by the area of the previous shape of the same kind.

----- Draw Lines -----
Click twice anywhere on the image to draw a straight line between two points.

Free: The line is unconstrained.
Parallel: The line is constrained parallel to the first constrained line drawn.
Perpendicular: The line is constrained perpendicular to the first constrained line drawn.
Angle: Type the desired angle in the text box. The line is constrained to that angle from the first constrained line drawn.

Length: Distance between the two points.
X Distance: Extent of the line along the specimen's X-axis (see Tools, Align, Align Specimen). Uses the image's X-axis if no alignment is set.
Y Distance: Extent of the line along the specimen's Y-axis. X and Y Distance are always positive, and together with Length form a right triangle.
Line Ratio: Length of the drawn line divided by the length of the previous line.
Angle: Clockwise angle from the previous line to the drawn line, from 0 to 360 degrees. Constrained lines are measured from the first constrained line.

----- Add Label -----
Put words, a dot or a star on the image. Choose which with the three buttons beneath Add Label, then click the image.

Text: Words you type. They begin at the point clicked, and a new label is ready to type in straight away.
Point: A filled black circle, centred on the point clicked.
Star: A filled black star, centred on the point clicked.

A label on the image is its mark and nothing else: no box, no border, and nothing behind it, so the picture shows through around it.
Rest the pointer on a label for a second and a half and its box appears: a border, a pale ground behind the mark, a bin off the top corner, a grip off the bottom corner and a shaded strip along the top edge. Words can only be typed once the box is out, so passing the pointer over a label never disturbs what it says. Move the pointer away and the box goes again, unless the label is being typed in.
A new label takes the font size and typeface set under Settings, Font, so labels match the rest of the writing on screen and a larger setting puts on a larger mark. Changing that setting afterwards changes the next label added; the labels already on the image keep the size and typeface they were given, and keep them when the project is saved and opened again.
Drag the corner grip to size a label: out for larger, in for smaller. For words this is the size of the lettering; for a dot or a star it is how wide the mark is drawn. Each label keeps its own size from then on, and for words the box takes the width of what is typed rather than the other way about.
Drag the strip along the top edge to move a label to another part of the image. A label cannot be dragged off the picture.
Click the bin to remove that label. Undo removes the last label added, Redo puts back the last label removed, and Clear Specimen Measurements removes every label on the specimen along with its measurements.
Labels belong to the specimen rather than to its measurements. Each specimen keeps its own, changing specimen puts that specimen's labels on screen and no others, and they are saved with the project.
Because a label annotates the picture rather than measuring it, it stays on screen whether See Previous Operations is on or off, it is not counted in the operation count, and it has no column in any table or export. The marks are included in Tools, Take Screenshot; the boxes and their handles are left out.
A label keeps its place on the image, so it stays on the feature it names when the window is resized, the view zoomed, or the image flipped or rotated.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OUTLINE MODE:

Click objects to generate an enclosed outline around them.

----- Automated Outline -----
Click an object to outline it. Several segmentation methods are tried and the best-scoring outline that contains the clicked point is kept. If an AI segmentation model is installed, it is tried first. All processing is done on the local computer.

Fill Sensitivity:
For objects on a solid background, set fill sensitivity to the maximum value. 
Higher fill sensitivity values allows greater variation of colors and shades within the outlined object, but also increases chances of capturing background on non-uniform backgrounds.

Multi-Click:
Click multiple times to expand the outline. 
Click outside of the region bounded by a dashed line to expand the outline to include that region. 
Click inside the region bounded by a dashed line to finalize the outline.

Watershed:
Higher computational expense leads to slower execution, but it tends to yield a more accurate outline.
Better-suited for complex images (e.g., multi-toned objects, patterned objects, and/or objects on a non-uniform background).
Boundary Sensitivity sets the amount of blur applied before watershed: Off, Low, or High.

Tip: If the outline is not accurate, try adjusting the fill sensitivity, or switch between default and watershed methods.
Tip: To reduce computational expense and increase performance speed, try cropping or decimating the image.

----- Draw by Hand -----
Click, hold the left mouse button, and drag to draw an outline. The outline closes automatically when the stroke crosses itself.

----- Erase, Push and Local Smooth -----
Each of these reaches around the cursor rather than following it exactly, so the cursor carries a see-through yellow disc the size of the brush. It shows how much of the outline will be taken in before anything is touched, and it keeps that size as the image is zoomed.

----- Erase -----
Click, hold the left mouse button, and drag anywhere on the outline to erase that part. Set the eraser size using the slider.
Alternately, hold the 'Alt' button and drag the cursor to erase part of the outline. This may be more convenient when using a trackpad.

----- Push -----
Click, hold the left mouse button, and drag anywhere on the outline to push that part outward. Set the brush size using the slider.
Alternately, hold the 'Alt' button and drag the cursor to push part of the outline. This may be more convenient when using a trackpad.

----- Smooth -----
Smooth a noisy outline to the desired amount by adjusting the slider value. The higher the number of passes, the more smoothing will occur.
Global smooth smooths the entire outline, while local smooth smooths only the area around the cursor.
For local smooth, set the brush size using the slider and the desired strength. Click, hold the left mouse button, and drag anywhere on the outline to smooth that part. 
Alternately, hold the 'Alt' button and drag the cursor to smooth part of the outline. This may be more convenient when using a trackpad.

----- Measure Outline -----
Before measuring, the outline is resampled to evenly spaced points. Without a scale, 512 points are used. With a scale, points are spaced by the Spacing value (default 0.05 units). The number of points is limited to between 64 and 4096.

Max Length: Maximum distance between any two points on the outline.
Max Width: Maximum extent of the outline perpendicular to Max Length.
Aspect Ratio (bbox): Width divided by height of the bounding box around the outline. The bounding box is aligned with the image edges.
Area: Area enclosed by the outline.
Perimeter: Length of the outline.
Circularity: 4π*Area / Perimeter^2
Solidity: Area of the outlined shape / Convex hull area
Convexity: Convex hull perimeter / Perimeter
Turn.Angles/Length: A measure of curvature. The sum of the turning angles (absolute change in angle of the tangent lines along the outline, in degrees) divided by the outline perimeter length. Approximates the average change in angle of the tangent lines along the outline.

Elliptic Fourier Analysis: Click to open a pop-up window to perform Elliptic Fourier Analysis (EFA) on the outline. The default number of harmonics is the smallest number that captures 99% of the cumulative harmonic power, and may be changed. A warning is shown if the first harmonic is too small or too circular for a reliable normalization.

Rotation sets what the coefficients are turned onto before they are compared. First harmonic turns each outline onto the major axis of its own first-harmonic ellipse: nothing has to be set up first, and it is the convention published coefficients are usually given in, but it takes each outline's orientation from its own shape, so two specimens held at different angles to their own anatomy come out the same way up. Drawn axis turns each outline onto the specimen's X axis instead (see Tools, Align, Align Specimen), which keeps the angle a specimen genuinely sits at relative to its anatomy, and keeps which side of that axis the shape leans to. Size and start point are normalized the same way under either choice. Choose Drawn axis on a specimen with no axis drawn and the first harmonic is used instead, with a warning saying so. The summary names the rotation used, and the angle when it came from a drawn axis, so coefficients measured against an axis that has since been redrawn can be told apart.

Commit Outline to History: Saves the outline and its metadata as an operation.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FILE:

----- Open Image ----- 
Resting on Open Image offers two kinds of file, 2D Image and 3D Model.

2D Image:
Open a 2D image file to analyze. Supported formats include .jpg, .png, .tif, and .bmp. 

3D Model:
Open a 3D file to analyze. PLY, OBJ, and STL files are supported. 
Position the model by pressing the scroll wheel and dragging, or by pressing the arrow keys. 
Click 'Capture View' to finish positioning the object. This will save the view as a 2D image, which the user can begin measuring.
'Apply to all 3D objects' gives every other 3D object in the session the same orientation and captures a view of each, so a sample can be posed once rather than one at a time.

Note: DinoLino is currently limited to 2D morphometrics. Measurements may only be taken on 2D projections of the 3D model.

----- Import Folder -----
Import a folder of images and 3D models to analyze. Incompatible file types will be ignored.
Folders that contain several large files may take several minutes to import.

----- Operation History -----
Resting on Operation History offers two things to do with what has been measured, Edit and Export.

Edit Operation History:
Opens the tables of everything measured this session, one tab per mode -- Curvature, Angle, Draw, Outline and EFA -- plus a Custom tab. A tab shows that mode's whole table: every variable of the mode as a column, every attempt as a row, so one row holds every measurement made on the same attempt.
Everything is done here. Read a table, delete a row or a whole specimen, hide a column, add a variable of your own from a formula, export the table on its own as a CSV, or stage it for the workbook. Deleting a row or a specimen is permanent and Ctrl + Z will not bring it back.

Export shapes:
A picker in the footer of the window, beside the button it governs. The same measurements can leave here in three shapes, and which one is wanted depends on what is going to read them.
Workbook, the one it opens on, writes the tables added to the workbook, one sheet each, exactly as they stand on screen.
Long format CSV writes a single file, suggested as geometric_data_long.csv, holding every mode at once -- one table, no sheets and no tabs. This is the shape ggplot2 and pandas read as they are given it.
One row carries one value, under these columns: Specimen, your group columns, spec_aligned, Replicate, Operation, Variable, Value and Unit. Operation names the tool the value came from -- Circular Arc, Triangle, Ellipse, Line -- so a variable two tools both measure is still told apart, and a variable of your own from a formula reads Formula with its mode in brackets, as Formula (Curvature). Replicate is the attempt number, so three arcs on one specimen are three replicates rather than three columns. It counts within one operation and not across them -- the first outline carrying metadata and the first outline carrying an EFA need not be the same outline -- so Specimen and Replicate together are not a safe key for joining one operation's rows to another's.
The unit has a column of its own, which keeps Value a number: a length reads 12.40 under Value and mm under Unit, not 12.40 mm in one cell. Degrees, ratios and counts arrive with Unit empty, since the tables record no unit for them. A ratio the geometry leaves undefined is left empty, so it reads as missing rather than as text. One word anywhere in Value would make the whole column text and silently break a plot drawn against it, which is why none is written.
A specimen's rows are kept together -- every mode for the first specimen, then every mode for the next. Empty cells are left out: a blank row would say only that a measurement was not taken, which its absence already says, and these tables are mostly blank. Hidden columns are left out, the same as in the wide shape. The Custom tab is not in the file, since every variable it shows is already there under the tool that measured it.
One row per specimen writes geometric_data_by_specimen.csv: every mode's variables side by side, one row to a specimen. This is the shape geomorph and Momocs ask for, which neither of the other two is -- both want one row or one array slice per specimen, and geomorph refuses inputs whose numbers of observations disagree. Note that neither package analyses measurements of this kind as shape: both are built on coordinates, so these variables serve as the covariates beside a shape analysis rather than as its input.
A variable's attempts are averaged, since one row cannot hold three arcs. Beside each operation is a count of how many attempts the specimen has of it -- circular_arc_n, triangle_n, outline_n -- so the mean of one attempt cannot be mistaken for the mean of five. The count is of readings taken, which is not always the number averaged: a length left out because no conversion reaches the file's unit was still a reading taken.
Only what was measured is in the file. Measure circular arcs and nothing else and the file holds the circular arc variables and their count, with no parabolic arc, spline, triangle, outline or EFA column at all. A tool no specimen was measured with contributes neither its variables nor its count, and a variable of a tool that was used but which stands empty for every specimen -- a harmonic deeper than any outline reached, a ratio the geometry leaves undefined -- is left out too. The tabs on screen are the other way round, every variable of a mode always a column, because there a column standing empty says plainly that nothing was drawn with that tool; in a file it says only that there is cleaning to do before the data can be read.
Every length and area in the file is in one unit, because a column heading can name only one and a column of millimetres for one specimen and centimetres for another is a wrong number rather than a wrong label. The unit is in the heading, as circ_radius_mm and tri_area_mm2. The conversion is done on the number, not on the calibration behind it, so a reading already rounded for display in its own unit keeps every digit it had: 0.06 cm comes through as 0.6 mm rather than as 0.00.
A specimen with no scale is measured in image pixels, which no real unit converts to, so its lengths and areas are blank while its ratios, angles and counts are there as usual. Image pixels are not offered as the file's unit while any specimen does have a scale, since choosing them would blank every specimen that has one. A session with no scale anywhere is written in image pixels, and then nothing is blank.
You are asked which unit to write in when the session holds more than one, and told what the pick costs when it costs something -- how many specimens have no scale -- before it is made rather than after the file is written. Where there is one unit and every specimen has it, nothing is asked.
A column of your own from a formula is left out when more than one unit had to be converted. A formula writes a bare number, so a column calculated from a length holds one specimen's millimetres beside another's centimetres with nothing left in the cell to tell them apart, and no heading could name a unit for it. The measured variables it was calculated from are all in the file and all in one unit, so the formula can be taken again over those. Where one unit covers the session, formula columns are kept as they are.
Whether the specimen is aligned, spec_aligned, is one column in both files, beside the specimen's name and its groups. It is a property of the specimen and not of anything measured on it, so it is one yes or no for a specimen however many tools were used and whatever was drawn with them, and it is never filed under a mode. It is read from the specimen rather than from a measurement, so it is answered even for a specimen measured only with tools that never look at it. The per-specimen file answers it for every specimen; the long file can only answer it for a specimen that has rows, since a specimen with nothing measured has none.
The tabs on screen read it inside the modes that use it, which is where it has to be read from: an angle taken from the specimen's axis means something different depending on it, so it belongs beside that angle on screen. It has no hide button there, as the group columns have none, because the two files write it of the specimen whatever a table is arranged to show.
The Export to CSV button on each tab is unaffected by the picker and always writes that tab's table as it stands on screen, which is what one table read on its own is good for.
The Custom tab joins ticked variables from any mode side by side. Columns may be added there and the Attempt heading retyped, but rows, specimens and columns are not deleted from it: a row there can belong to several modes at once, so there is no one measurement for a delete to mean.

Export Operation History:
Export the history of all operations performed. Undone or cleared operations will not be included. The exported file will be a workbook in .xlsx format, one sheet per mode.
This one is the complete record, so it writes every column even where a column has been hidden. The Geometric Data workbook writes the tables as you have arranged them, hidden columns left out; use that one when the arrangement is the point, and this one when nothing should be missing.

----- Save Settings -----
Keep the current settings for the next session. Most of them are in the Settings menu, but a few sit with the commands they belong to -- View Axes under Tools, Align, and View Scalebar under Tools, Scale -- and those are kept too.

----- Restore Default Settings -----
Return every remembered setting to its default, wherever its menu item lives.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
EDIT:

----- Undo ----- 
Undoes the last operation performed. This removes the drawn elements and data associated with that operation. It will not be stored in the operation history.

----- Redo ----- 
Redoes the most recently undone operation. This reinstates the drawn elements and data associated with that operation.

----- Clear Specimen Measurements -----
Takes every measurement and every label off the specimen on screen. Asks first, and cannot be undone. Also on the Clear Specimen Measurements button and on Ctrl + Shift + C; see Global Methods for the detail.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOOLS:

----- Scale -----
Holds the scale commands: Set Scale, View Scalebar, and the two that pass a scale to other specimens. Everything but Set Scale is greyed out until a scale is set, since without one there is nothing to show and nothing to pass on.

----- Set Scale -----
Click on two points in the image to draw a line. Enter the real-world length of the line in the input box and click "OK" to set the scale. 
This will allow the software to convert pixel measurements to real-world units. Available units are km, hm, dam, m, dm, cm, mm, micrometers, and nm.
Each specimen keeps its own scale. Measurements are shown as Unscaled until a scale is set.

----- View Scalebar -----
Toggle on or off to display a scalebar in the workspace. The unit it reads in is printed at the end of the bar.
Unzoomed, the bar reads in the unit the specimen was scaled in. Zoomed, the unit follows the view: zooming in reads in a smaller unit and zooming out in a larger one, taken from the same list Set Scale offers, so the numbers stay easy to count at any magnification.
The bar is divided into ten segments, alternating black and white, with a tick rising out of it under each of the eleven numbers so a number can be read against the exact place it marks. Its length on screen is taken from the scale and from how far the image is zoomed in, so it reads true at any magnification and lengthens as you zoom in.
The numbers read 0 to 10 wherever ten of the unit fits the view at a readable width. Where it does not, each segment counts more than one unit, and the numbers say so: 0, 5, 10 and on to 50. Unzoomed the unit is held to the one you scaled in, so there a segment may instead count a fraction of one: 0, 0.5, 1.0 and on to 5.0. The division is taken again whenever the zoom or the window changes.
A grip at the right end of the bar sets its length. Drag it sideways and the span changes with it, so a longer bar covers more units and a shorter one fewer. The length snaps to one the scale can honestly show, so the bar steps between readings rather than following the pointer exactly. Only sideways travel counts: the bar keeps its height. Double-click the grip to hand the length back to the view.
This is not a Settings switch and is not kept by Save Settings, because it belongs to a specimen that has a scale rather than to the program.
The scalebar may be dragged anywhere in the workspace, and is included in Tools, Take Screenshot. The grip is left out of the screenshot, the way the cursor is.

----- Apply Scale to All Specimens -----
Gives every other specimen the scale of the one on screen. One photographic setup usually serves a whole tray of specimens, so a scale measured once on one of them is the scale for all of them, and there is no reason to measure it again on each.
A specimen that already has a scale measured on its own image is not written over without being asked about. The question offers Overwrite or Leave Them, and a tick box to stop it being asked again; Cancel leaves every specimen exactly as it was. The choice is remembered for the rest of the session, and between sessions when File, Save Settings is on.
A specimen already carrying a scale passed on from somewhere else is replaced without asking, since nothing measured is lost by it.
When the command finishes it says how many specimens took the scale and how many kept their own.

----- Apply Scale to Selected Specimens -----
The same, for the specimens ticked in Directory, Sample rather than all of them. Greyed out until at least one is ticked there. The specimen on screen is never a target, so its measured scale is never marked as passed on.

----- Scales that were passed on -----
A specimen given another specimen's scale is marked as having inherited it, so a reader can always tell which specimens were actually measured. The mark appears in two places: beside the specimen's name in Directory, Sample, and on the sidebar beneath the specimen name, which reads Scale: inherited.
An inherited scale is a working scale in every other respect: it converts measurements, drives the scalebar, and is saved with the project. Measuring a scale on the specimen itself with Set Scale replaces it and clears the mark.

----- Align -----
Holds the two alignment commands, Align Specimen and View Axes, the way Scale holds its own. Greyed out until a picture is loaded, since neither has anything to act on without one.

----- Align Specimen -----
Set the specimen's X or Y axis by clicking two points on the image. The other axis is set perpendicular to it. Used for X Distance and Y Distance in Draw Mode, for Axis Angle, and for the Drawn axis rotation in Elliptic Fourier Analysis.
Rotating or flipping the image removes the alignment.

----- View Axes -----
Toggle on or off to show the specimen's X and Y axes as a compass in the corner of the workspace. Drag the compass to move it.
The compass needs a picture loaded, since it describes how a specimen sits in its own image. It goes off screen when the picture does and comes back with the next one; the tick itself is left as you set it, and is kept for the next session while File, Save Settings is on.
This was in the Settings menu in earlier versions. It sits here now, beside the command that sets the axes, the way View Scalebar sits beside Set Scale.

----- Image Adjustments -----
Adjust brightness, contrast, or saturation of the uploaded image, from -100 to 100. Zero is the default value for all parameters. Each specimen keeps its own settings, and Reset returns them all to zero.
These change only how the picture is shown. No measurement reads them, and nothing is written to the image file. Decimate, which does change the image, is a separate command.

----- Decimate -----
Decimate the image by a specified factor to reduce its resolution and computational expense. This may be helpful for large images or when using the watershed method in outline mode. Enter a target pixel count or percentage.

----- Flip -----
Flip image horizontally or vertically to produce a mirror image.

----- Rotate -----
Rotate image by 90 degrees clockwise or counterclockwise.

----- Reposition 3D Object -----
Return to the 3D image viewer after capturing view of a 3D image to reposition the object. Click 'Capture View' when ready to save the new view as a 2D image, which the user can begin measuring.

----- Take Screenshot ----- 
Take a screenshot of the current software image screen. The screenshot may be saved as a .jpg, .png, or .tif file. The screenshot will include all drawn elements. 

----- Image Cache -----
Resting on Image Cache offers two choices, Clear and Edit. Neither removes any measurement.

Clear:
Remove all images from the cache without removing associated measurements.

Edit:
Review all cached images and remove them one at a time, without removing associated measurements.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SETTINGS:

Adjust various properties using the Settings drop-down menu items.

----- See Tips ----- 
Toggle on or off to see advice for using the program displayed at the bottom of the screen. Guidelines are also available in the user guide.

----- See Previous Operations ----- 
Toggle on or off to keep drawn elements of previous operations on the screen.

----- See Operation Count -----
View the number of operations performed for each specimen. 
Toggle on to display an on-screen window which contains the number of circular arcs, parabolic arcs, splines, triangles, rectangles, squares, ellipses, circles, lines, and outlines drawn for a given specimen. A kind with nothing drawn yet is left out of the window.
Opening a new specimen resets count to zero for all operations. Undo and Redo also affect operation count.
The Operation Count window may be dragged anywhere in the workspace.

----- See Navigation Window -----
Toggle on or off to display a navigation window in the top left corner of the screen. 
The navigation window shows a zoomed-out view of the image, with a rectangle indicating the current viewable area. 
While zoomed in, click anywhere in the navigation window to move the viewable area to that location.

----- See Data Workshop -----
Two rows. Geometric Data opens the operation history for editing, and exports every staged table as one .xlsx workbook. 2D Outlines opens the gallery of stored outline shapes, and exports them as silhouette images. Toggle on or off to display on the right sidebar.
One mode's table on its own is read, edited and exported from its own tab in the operation history, which is the one place any of that happens.

Groups:
Tick specimens in the Sample tab and click Add to group to put them in a named group. A group is a column of its own: it appears after Specimen and Attempt in every table and in every export, and a specimen may belong to one group per column. Column and group names are capped at 14 characters.

Hidden Columns:
Each mode tab of the Operation History window marks every measurement column with a cross that hides it; the Custom tab has none, since its columns are ticked and unticked on the left instead. A hidden column leaves the tab, its CSV and its workbook sheet, but keeps its measurements, and Restore hidden columns on that tab brings them all back. Group columns belong to the specimen rather than the table and cannot be hidden.
Because the tab and its exports are the same table, a column hidden on screen is a column missing from the file. Nothing in the file records that, so the hidden ones are named under the table while any are hidden, and clicking one brings back just that column. File, Export Operation History is the exception: it writes every column regardless.

The Workbook:
Each tab of the Operation History window has an Add to workbook button. Staged tables are counted in the footer of that window and are the ones written when the workbook is exported, either from the Export workbook button in that footer or from the Geometric Data row of the Data Workshop. With nothing staged, every table is written instead, one sheet per mode.
A project saved by an earlier version recorded its staged sheets under the old per-tool names. Those are matched to the mode that now holds them when the project is opened, so the staging survives.

Formula Columns:
On any tab of the Operation History window, click Add column to make a new variable calculated from that table's columns. Enter a name of up to 14 characters and a formula, such as = outline_area / outline_perim^2. Column names may be typed straight into the formula, or found in the list beside it and inserted.
A column you have made carries a pencil on its header, which opens the formula again to change or delete it. A formula naming a column its table does not have is listed under the table instead of calculated, so it can still be corrected.
Angle Mode's three triangle angles were called tri_anglea, tri_angleb and tri_anglec in earlier versions and are now angle_a, angle_b and angle_c. A project saved under the old names keeps its ticked variables and its hidden columns, which are matched up when it opens; a formula written with an old name is reported on opening and needs the new one typed in.
A column name in a formula stands for that column's value on the same row. Formulas use addition, subtraction, multiplication, division and powers, with brackets to group them. Comparisons are written = <> < <= > >= and give 1 or 0, as in IF(outline_area > 100, 1, 0).
Row functions: SUM, PRODUCT, AVERAGE, MIN, MAX, COUNT, MEDIAN, ABS, SQRT, LN, LOG, LOG10, EXP, POWER, MOD, ROUND, INT, SIGN, PI, SIN, COS, TAN, ASIN, ACOS, ATAN, ATAN2, DEGREES, RADIANS, IF, IFBLANK, ISBLANK.
Whole-column functions, taken over every specimen: COLSUM, COLMEAN, COLMIN, COLMAX, COLCOUNT, COLMEDIAN, COLSD.
Specimen functions, taken over the row's own specimen: SPECSUM, SPECMEAN, SPECMIN, SPECMAX, SPECCOUNT, SPECMEDIAN, SPECSD.
Each of these takes one column name, as in SPECMEAN(outline_area). COLSD and SPECSD are sample standard deviations, which need at least two values.
A blank cell is not zero. Any arithmetic using a blank cell gives a blank result, so a row without that measurement is left empty rather than counted as zero. SUM, PRODUCT, AVERAGE, MIN, MAX, COUNT and MEDIAN skip blank arguments, and IFBLANK(value, 0) puts a number in their place. A calculation with no numerical answer, such as division by zero, shows #ERR.
Cells carry their units, such as 4.56 cm, and a formula reads the number in front of the unit. Each specimen's measurements are converted with that specimen's own scale, so a table spanning specimens scaled in different units shows each of them correctly in its own.
Results are plain numbers rounded to 4 decimal places, and the unit is dropped when a cell is read for a formula, a plot, or a Principal Component Analysis. So any of those drawing on specimens scaled in different units is mixing those units together, without saying so, and it is worth scaling a sample in one unit throughout. Apply Scale to All Specimens is the quickest way to do that.
A row holds attempt n of every operation kind in its table, so a formula combining two kinds combines their nth attempts.
Formula columns are recalculated whenever a table is built, and appear in the table, in its CSV and workbook exports, in the History window, and among the variables offered in the Plot tab and Principal Component Analysis. A formula may use any formula column made before it. The pencil button on a column header edits or deletes the column, and the X button hides it as for any other column.
Formula columns last for the session and are removed by File, New Project.

Custom Table:
The History window has a Custom tab that builds one table from variables of any mode. Tick the variables on the left; each one is added as a column in the order it is ticked, and Clear all empties the choice.
Rows follow the same rule as a single mode's table: attempt 1 of every kind shares a row, attempt 2 the next, and a kind with fewer attempts leaves blank cells. Specimen and group columns are included as in every other table.
Add column writes a formula column for this table, so measurements of different modes can be combined, for example = outline_area / tri_area. These columns are edited from the Formula columns list on the left.
Export to CSV writes the table as custom_data.csv, and Add to workbook includes it in the exported workbook as a sheet named Custom. The choice of variables lasts for the session and is removed by File, New Project.

EFA Data:
The coefficients table is read, edited and exported from the EFA tab of the Operation History window, and it is a sheet of the Geometric Data workbook. It may also be exported from the Elliptic Fourier Analysis window.

2D Outlines:
Outlines kept with Commit Outline to History may be exported as black silhouettes on a white background. Outlines are automatically filled prior to export. An outline that was only drawn is not exported; only the ones committed are, and they are the snapshots taken at commit time, so undoing or editing an outline afterwards does not change what comes out.
Committed outlines may be renamed, duplicated and removed one at a time from the gallery, which the Edit button on the 2D Outlines row opens. Only what survives there is exported.
What is stored is the outline itself -- the traced coordinates, kept in the project file. What the export writes is a picture of it, a filled silhouette in one of the formats below, which is what a figure needs. The numbers describing the shape are the EFA coefficients and the outline measurements, in their own tabs.
Select a destination folder for export, a file type (.png, .tif, .bmp, .jpg, or .svg), and desired canvas size.
Scale: Off by default, which exports every outline at the size it was traced. Switched on, each outline is resized so the filled shape covers 25% of the canvas.
	   Some elongated shapes are exceptions to this. If an elongated shape at target area reaches past the margins, it is shrunk until it fits within the canvas.
Align: Outlines may be rotated onto the long axis to align orientations, or exported in original orientation.

----- See Directory -----
Navigate file paths, view, and edit the specimens uploaded for a given a session.

Files:
Browse folders and open images or 3D models.
The working directory can be set three ways: right-click a folder and choose Set Working Directory, click a folder and then click Set, or type or paste a path into the box and click Set or press Enter. Clicking a folder puts its path in the box, so what Set is about to apply is always the path on screen.

Sample:
View, load, duplicate, and remove specimens in the session, assign them to groups, and reposition a 3D object to capture a new view.

Plot:
Plot recorded measurements as a scatter plot, boxplot, dot plot, histogram, or QQ plot, and export the plot as an image.
Principal Component Analysis (PCA): Add two or more variables, then run the analysis. Variables are centered on their means. If standardization is selected, each variable is also divided by its standard deviation (correlation-matrix PCA).

----- See Rex -----
A friend to help you work.

----- Line Options ----- 
Opens a window holding the color and the thickness every mode draws at. Both take effect as they are chosen, so the window can be left open while the change is judged against the image underneath.

Color: Adjust color of drawn elements. Multiple options are available to accommodate colorblindness and ensure operation visibility on any image.

Thickness: Adjust the width of drawn elements, from 0.5 to 10. A thin line sits closer to a feature's edge on a large photograph; a thick one stays visible on a small or busy one.

With See Previous Operations switched on, the measurements already on screen are drawn again in the new style. The guide marks a tool shows while a measurement is being made keep their own weight, since they say where a measurement will go rather than being part of one.

----- Font ----- 
Adjust font size or font family. Dyslexia-friendly fonts are available. 
If needed, you may accommodate very large font sizes by resizing the sidebar or program window with clicking and dragging.
The font size and typeface also set how a new label from Draw mode's Add Label starts out. Labels already on an image are not changed by it.

----- Column names and abbreviations -----

Column names in data tables correspond to the following: 

"Specimen" = Specimen Name
"Attempt" = Operation Count

In Curvature Mode:
"circ_centangle" = Central Angle of Circular Arc
"circ_chordarc" = Chord-Arc Ratio of Circular Arc
"circ_risespan" = Rise-Span Ratio of Circular Arc
"circ_radius" = Radius of Circular Arc
"para_chordarc" = Chord-Arc Ratio of Parabolic Arc
"para_risespan" = Rise-Span Ratio of Parabolic Arc
"para_vertcurv" = Vertex Curvature of Parabolic Arc
"para_radius" = Vertex Radius of Parabolic Arc
"spline_turnangle" = Turning Angle / Spline Length Ratio of n-Point Spline
"spline_tortuosity" = Total length divided by chord length of n-Point Spline
"spline_length" = Length of n-Point Spline

In Angle Mode:
"angle_a" = Angle A of Triangle
"angle_b" = Angle B of Triangle
"angle_c" = Angle C of Triangle
"tri_aspect" = Aspect Ratio of Triangle
"tri_area" = Area of Triangle
"axis_angle" = Angle from the specimen's axis to the line drawn, clockwise, in degrees
"spec_aligned" = Whether the specimen has an axis set. A property of the specimen, so it reads the same wherever it appears

In Draw Mode:
"rect_aspect" = Aspect Ratio of Rectangle
"rect_area" = Area of Rectangle
"sqr_area" = Area of Square
"ellipse_aspect" = Aspect Ratio of Ellipse
"ellipse_area" = Area of Ellipse
"circ_area" = Area of Circle
"line_length" = Line Length
"line_xdist" = X Distance of Line
"line_ydist" = Y Distance of Line
"line_ratio" = Ratio of line length relative to previous line length
"line_angle" = Angle of line relative to previous line
"spec_aligned" = Whether the specimen has an axis set. A property of the specimen, so it reads the same wherever it appears

In Outline Mode:
"outline_maxlength" = Maximum Length of Outline
"outline_maxwidth" = Maximum Width of Outline
"outline_aspect" = Outline Aspect Ratio
"outline_perim" = Outline Perimeter Length
"outline_area" = Area of Filled Outline
"outline_circ" = Outline Circularity
"outline_solidity" = Outline Solidity
"outline_convexity" = Outline Convexity
"outline_sumturn" = Sum of Turning Angles along Outline
"outline_turnlength" = Ratio of Sum of Turning Angles / Outline Perimeter Length
"outline_spacing" = Spacing between resampled outline points
"outline_points" = Number of resampled outline points used for measurement
"outline_vertices" = Number of points in the drawn outline
"outline_hasmeta" = Whether metadata was generated for the outline
"efa_harmonics" = Number of Elliptic Fourier Analysis harmonics
"efa_a1", "efa_b1", "efa_c1", "efa_d1", ... = The four Elliptic Fourier coefficients of each harmonic, one set per harmonic

Columns added with Add column appear under the name given to them, after the measurement columns.
